# rdfine

`rdfine` is a small Python package that provides ergonomic abstractions on top of
[`rdflib`](https://rdflib.readthedocs.io/) and
[`pyld`](https://github.com/digitalbazaar/pyld) for working with RDF graphs and
JSON-LD documents. It is used by the pipeline generator to read, transform,
filter, and serialize RDF-based pipeline definitions.

The package is organized around three main classes and a small set of utility
functions.

## Overview

| Module | Exported symbols | Purpose |
| --- | --- | --- |
| [graph_reader.py](graph_reader.py) | `GraphReader` | Read-only / functional view over an RDF graph with SPARQL, filtering, traversal and inference helpers. |
| [graph_dict.py](graph_dict.py) | `GraphDict` | JSON-LD-shaped dict view of a graph, supporting framing, path-based access and serialization. |
| [prefix_store.py](prefix_store.py) | `PrefixStore`, `PrefixConflictError` | Registry of namespace prefixes plus string-level `compact` / `expand` / `drop`. Rejects prefix/URL collisions by default. |
| [prefix_apply.py](prefix_apply.py) | (internal) | Polymorphic `apply_prefixes(data, store, action)` dispatcher across `str` / `dict` / `list` / `DataFrame`, plus `include_in_query` for SPARQL strings. |
| [rdflib_bridge.py](rdflib_bridge.py) | (internal) | rdflib glue: `bind_to_namespace`, `node_to_python`, `python_to_node`. |
| [utils.py](utils.py) | `load_yaml`, `drop_empty`, `parse_config` | YAML loading and JSON-LD/dict cleanup helpers. |

All public symbols are re-exported from the package root:

```python
from rdfine import (
    GraphReader,
    GraphDict,
    PrefixStore,
    PrefixConflictError,
    load_yaml,
    drop_empty,
    parse_config,
)
```

See [__init__.py](__init__.py) for the full export list.

## Installation / dependencies

`rdfine` is a plain Python package (no `setup.py`/`pyproject.toml` of its own in
this folder). It expects the following third-party packages to be available in
the environment:

- `rdflib`
- `pyld`
- `pandas`
- `pyyaml`
- `boltons`
- `glom`
- `validators`

## Classes

### `PrefixStore` ([prefix_store.py](prefix_store.py))

A registry of `prefix -> URL` mappings backed by a plain `dict[str, str]`,
ordered alphabetically for stable display. It can be constructed from:

- an `rdflib.Graph` — prefixes are read from the graph's namespaces
- a `dict` — copied as-is

Main capabilities:

- **String-only fast path** (returns `str`):
  - `compact_string(url)` — longest-match prefix compaction
  - `expand_string(url)` — expand `prefix:local` to a full URL
  - `drop_string(url)` — strip a known prefix, returning only the local part
- **Polymorphic apply** (return type follows the input):
  - `apply_prefixes(data, action)` — the canonical dispatcher; `action` is
    `"compact"`, `"expand"`, or `"drop"`. Accepts `str`, `dict`, `list`, or
    `pandas.DataFrame`.
  - `compact(data)` / `expand(data)` / `drop(data)` — convenience aliases
    for the three actions above.
- **Graph integration**:
  - `bind_to_namespace(graph)` — register all stored prefixes on an `rdflib.Graph`
  - `include_in_query(query)` — prepend `PREFIX` declarations to a SPARQL
    query, skipping declarations already present
- **Type conversion** between rdflib nodes and native Python types via
  `node_to_python` / `python_to_node`.
- **Registry maintenance**:
  - `load(source, replace=True)` — ingest from a `Graph` or `dict`. Raises
    `PrefixConflictError` when an incoming `(prefix, url)` pair clashes with
    an existing one. Re-registering an identical pair is a no-op.
  - `fetch_prefix(node_id)` — look up which known prefix (if any) a string
    uses. Longest-URL match wins.
  - `replace_prefix_in_store(original, replacement)` — rename a stored
    prefix; raises `PrefixConflictError` if the replacement is already taken
    for a different URL.

Longer URLs take precedence over shorter ones during compaction (see
`_order_prefixes`).

The heavy traversal logic (dicts, lists, DataFrames) and rdflib glue live in
separate sibling modules ([prefix_apply.py](prefix_apply.py) and
[rdflib_bridge.py](rdflib_bridge.py)). The methods above are thin forwarders
so callers don't need to know about that split.

#### `PrefixConflictError`

Raised by `load` and `replace_prefix_in_store` when an incoming entry would
clash with an existing one. The exception carries:

- `kind` — `"prefix_collision"` (same prefix, different URL) or
  `"url_collision"` (same URL, different prefix)
- `existing` — the `(prefix, url)` pair already in the store
- `incoming` — the `(prefix, url)` pair that triggered the conflict

### `GraphReader` ([graph_reader.py](graph_reader.py))

An immutable wrapper around an `rdflib.Graph`. Every transformation returns a
new `GraphReader` instance, leaving the original untouched.

Construction (single consolidated constructor):

```python
reader = GraphReader(graph)                          # from an rdflib.Graph
reader = GraphReader(graph, prefix_store=my_store)   # reuse an existing PrefixStore
reader = GraphReader(df, prefix_store=store)         # from a triples DataFrame + PrefixStore
```

Key properties and methods:

- `graph` — the underlying `rdflib.Graph`
- `prefix_store` — a `PrefixStore` populated from the graph's namespaces
- `df` — cached `pandas.DataFrame` view of all triples
  (columns include `sub`, `pred`, `obj`, `sub_type`, `obj_type`)
- `add(graph)` / `remove(graph)` — return a new reader with triples added/removed
- `filter(sub=, pred=, obj=, sub_type=, obj_type=, action="keep"|"drop", regex=False)` —
  triple-pattern filtering with optional regex matching. The plain (non-regex,
  no type filter) path uses `rdflib.Graph.triples` directly for speed.
- `rename(old, new)` — rename a node across subjects and objects
- `check_exists(node_id)` — boolean check whether a node appears as subject,
  predicate, or object
- `select(select_clause, where)` — run a SPARQL `SELECT` query; returns a
  `DataFrame`.
- `construct(construct_clause, where)` — run a SPARQL `CONSTRUCT` query;
  returns a new `GraphReader`.
- `ask(where)` — run a SPARQL `ASK` query; returns a `bool`.
- `sparql(query)` — run a raw SPARQL string; result type depends on the
  query (`DataFrame` / `GraphReader` / `bool`).
- `infer(filepath, max_repetitions=10)` — apply a YAML rule file of
  `construct` / `where` SPARQL rules until a fixed point is reached
- `traverse(node_id, direction="along"|"against"|"both", exclude=, along=, against=, prune=)` —
  recursively extract a sub-graph starting from a node
- `serialize(output_format)` — thin wrapper around `graph.serialize`

### `GraphDict` ([graph_dict.py](graph_dict.py))

A JSON-LD-shaped, dict-based view of a graph. Even list-shaped graphs are wrapped
in a `{"@graph": [...]}` envelope so the value is always a dict.

Construction (single consolidated constructor):

```python
gd = GraphDict(input_dict)                       # dict must contain @context
gd = GraphDict(input_dict, prefix_store=store)   # explicit prefix store
gd = GraphDict(rdflib_graph)                     # build from an rdflib.Graph
```

Key properties and methods:

- `dict` — the underlying compacted JSON-LD dict
- `prefix_store` — the associated `PrefixStore`
- `graph` — re-materialize the data as an `rdflib.Graph`
- `frame(jsonld_frame)` — apply a JSON-LD frame, returning a new `GraphDict`
- `get(path)` — extract a value via a [`glom`](https://glom.readthedocs.io/)
  path; dicts are wrapped in a new `GraphDict`
- `set(path, new_value, sep=".")` — strict, non-creating path update; returns a
  new `GraphDict`
- `find(path_pattern=None, value_pattern=None)` — regex search over a flattened
  `path` / `value` DataFrame index of the dict (cached after first call)
- `serialize(output_format, prefix_action="compact"|"expand"|"drop"|None)` —
  serialize the dict to `"json"` or `"yaml"`/`"yml"`

`get` and `set` accept the same path forms: a `glom.Path`, a list/tuple of
segments, or a dot-separated string. Numeric string segments are auto-converted
to integers so they index into lists — matching the path format produced by
`find`.

#### Graph conversion is always safe

Accessing `.graph` never raises on unregistered prefixes. Any key whose
prefix isn't known is patched on-the-fly with the placeholder prefix
``na_`` (mapped to ``https://no_prefix_available.com/``), and a fresh
`PrefixStore` extended with that mapping is used for the rdflib
conversion. Neither the original `dict` nor the original `PrefixStore`
are mutated — the placeholder lives only on the returned `Graph`.

This means you can build, mutate, find, frame, and serialize
(JSON / YAML) a `GraphDict` with bare keys without any prefix gymnastics;
the placeholder only appears when you ask for the graph form.

## Utilities ([utils.py](utils.py))

- `load_yaml(filename)` — read a YAML file with `yaml.safe_load`; any
  `yaml.YAMLError` raised during parsing is propagated to the caller.
- `drop_empty(obj, empty_values=None)` — recursively remove key/value pairs
  whose value is considered empty. ``empty_values`` defaults to
  ``[None, [], {}, ""]``.
- `parse_config(input_dict)` — parse a dict containing a `tcs:literal` (YAML
  string) or `tcs:embedded` (already-structured) configuration into a
  `":config"` key.

## Usage example

```python
from rdflib import Graph
from rdfine import GraphReader, GraphDict

g = Graph()
g.parse("pipeline.ttl", format="turtle")

# Functional, query-oriented view
reader = GraphReader(g)
subset = reader.filter(pred="rdf:type", obj="tcs:Processor")
df = subset.df

# JSON-LD / dict-oriented view
gd = GraphDict(g)
print(gd.serialize("yaml", prefix_action="compact"))
```
